[:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/782"} "Olympiako Kleisto Gymnastirio, Olympiako Athletiko Kentro Athinon Spiros Loues, Maroussi"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/70905"} "83 from 30 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The format was the same as it had been from 1984-2000, with eight gymnasts advancing from the team all-around, but no more than two per nation. The 2003 World Champion was Brazilian " [:a {:href "/athletes/107180"} "Daiane dos Santos"] " who qualified for the final but went out of bounds on her first tumbling pass and stumbled on her second. Her score of 9.375 would place her only fifth. Romanian " [:a {:href "/athletes/107141"} "Cătălina Ponor"] " had won a gold medal on beam only a few minutes before, and performed brilliantly on floor to win her second apparatus gold medal. Her score of 9.750 gave her a 0.188 margin over her teammate " [:a {:href "/athletes/107143"} "Dana Şofronie"] ", the largest margin of victory ever on floor exercise, and the largest at the Olympics in an apparatus event since 1976 when " [:a {:href "/athletes/28995"} "Nadia Comăneci"] " won the uneven bars by 0.2 points. Spain’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/107175"} "Patricia Moreno"] " won the bronze medal, the first Olympic medal ever won by a Spanish gymnast."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Points"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "383"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/107141"} "Cătălina Ponor"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ROU"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ROU.png"}] "ROU"]] [:td {} "9.750"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "385"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/1